    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Verdict\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away.** You should **negotiate**. \n\nBecause Google recruited you while you are content at Meta, you hold maximum leverage. While the headline number looks appealing due to Google\u2019s front-loaded equity structure, the initial offer takes a step backward on your base salary and leaves significant money on the table for an L6 (Staff / Senior PM) role.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### 1. How the Google Equity Actually Works\nGoogle uses a front-loaded **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** vesting schedule for new-hire equity grants:\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity (RSU/GSU)** | ~$250,000 | $356,400 *(33%)* | $356,400 *(33%)* | $237,600 *(22%)* | $129,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Total Annual Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$638,400** | **~$519,600** | **~$411,600** |\n\n*(Note: Google figures exclude potential refresher grants and stock appreciation.)*\n\n#### 2. Key Observations\n* **Year 1 & 2 Upside:** You get a healthy **+$112k/yr bump** over your Meta comp for the first two years due to the 33% front-load.\n* **The Base Salary Cut:** Google offered **$235k**, which is below your current **$240k** base and near the midpoint of their posted range ($183k\u2013$271k).\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** For external L6 PM hires\u2014especially passive candidates being poached from Meta\u2014a first-year sign-on bonus is standard.\n* **Year 3 & 4 Cliff:** Google\u2019s initial grant drops sharply in Year 3 ($238k) and Year 4 ($130k). Google relies on annual refreshers (which typically vest 25%/yr over 4 years) to bridge this gap, but because refreshers depend entirely on performance ratings and calibration, you should secure more upfront guaranteed value.\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy: What to Ask For\n\nGoogle rarely rescinds offers for polite, well-reasoned counter-offers. Your goal is to increase base salary to avoid a pay cut, secure a sign-on bonus to bridge transition friction, and push the equity grant toward top-of-band L6 levels.\n\n#### Specific Ask:\n\n| Item | Google Offer | Your Counter-Ask | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Top half of their $183k\u2013$271k band; ensures a clean increase over your current $240k base. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving your stable pipeline of Meta refreshers and forfeiting unvested equity/annual bonus cycles. |\n| **Equity (GSUs)** | $1,080,000 / 4 yrs | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 / 4 yrs** | Standard top-tier band for Bay Area L6 PM. At $1.25M, Year 1 equity becomes ~$412k. |\n\n---\n\n### How to Script the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone or email with a positive, collaborative tone:\n\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer. I've really enjoyed meeting the team, and I'm excited about the scope of the L6 PM role.*\n>\n> *As mentioned when we first connected, I'm very happy and well-compensated at Meta, where I'm currently at $240k base and ~$526k total annual compensation with a strong vesting pipeline. Because I wasn't actively looking to move, making a transition needs to make clear financial sense across the entire package.*\n>\n> *To close this out and sign happily, here is what would get me across the line:*\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** An adjustment to **$255,000**, ensuring I don't take a step back on base pay relative to my current level and within the posted band.*\n> 2. * **Sign-on Bonus:** A **$75,000\u2013$100,000** sign-on bonus to offset the unvested equity and bonus timing I\u2019d be leaving on the table.*\n> 3. * **Equity:** An increase of the equity grant to **$1,250,000** over 4 years to reflect senior market rates for an L6 PM.*\n>\n> *If we can get close to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*\n\n### Final Decision Rule\n* **If they meet you halfway** (e.g., bump base to $250k+, add a $50k\u2013$75k sign-on, and bump equity slightly): **Accept**, assuming you are excited about the team/product scope.\n* **If they completely refuse to move on all dimensions**: You hold all the cards. Stay at Meta where your career and equity momentum are established, and let Google know the door remains open for future opportunities.",
