     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n**Yes, financially this is a compelling offer and you should strongly consider taking it.** \n\nYour negotiation was successful: you extracted an extra **$200,000 in equity** (pushing the grant from $1.08M to $1.28M) and brought base pay up to parity with your Meta salary ($240k). \n\nAt **~$710,000 in Year 1 and Year 2**, this is at the upper end of the band for an L6 Product Manager in the Bay Area.\n\n---\n\n### The Updated Math Breakdown\n\nBecause Google uses a front-loaded **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** vesting schedule, here is how your compensation looks year over year:\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $36,000 *(15%)* | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Equity (GSU)** | ~$250,000 | $422,400 *(33%)* | $422,400 *(33%)* | $281,600 *(22%)* | $153,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Total Annual Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$710,400** | **~$710,400** | **~$569,600** | **~$441,600** |\n\n*(Note: Google figures exclude future annual refreshers and stock price appreciation.)*\n\n---\n\n### Addressing the Trade-offs\n\n#### 1. The $200k in unvested Meta RSUs\nWhile you didn't get a separate cash sign-on bonus, **Google effectively converted that into upfront equity**. \n* At Meta, you would vest ~$200,000 of RSUs in the next 12 months.\n* At Google, you will vest **$422,400** in Year 1.\n* In Year 1 alone, you will take home **+$184,400 more** than your current run rate at Meta. Over the first two years combined, you will make roughly **+$368,000 more** than staying at Meta.\n\n#### 2. Years 3 and 4 Equity Drop\nThe grant drops to $281.6k in Year 3 and $153.6k in Year 4. However:\n* Even *without* any refreshers, Year 3 total comp ($569.6k) still beats your current Meta comp ($526k).\n* Google awards annual equity refreshers during review cycles. Because each refresher typically vests evenly over 4 years (25%/yr), grants earned in Year 1 and Year 2 will begin stacking on top of your base grant by Year 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### How to Decide\n\nSince the financial compensation is resolved, the decision comes down to qualitative factors:\n\n#### Reasons to **Accept**:\n1. **The Comp Bump is Real:** Earning ~$710k/yr for the next 24 months represents a major step up in net income.\n2. **Resume Optionality:** Holding Senior/Staff PM (L6) titles at both Meta and Google provides strong career leverage across Big Tech.\n3. **Pace and Sustainable Growth:** Google\u2019s culture generally offers different working dynamics and scope scales that many PMs find valuable after several years in Meta's fast-paced cycle.\n\n#### Reasons to **Stay at Meta**:\n1. **Resetting Social Capital:** You stated you were happy at Meta. Leaving means resetting your reputation, trust network, and product context from scratch.\n2. **Promotion Velocity:** If you are within 12\u201318 months of an IC7 (Director/Principal PM) promotion at Meta, jumping to Google might delay that timeline.\n3. **Execution Speed:** If you thrive on Meta\u2019s direct, bottom-up execution style, Google\u2019s consensus-heavy product culture can sometimes feel slower to navigate.\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n**Accept the offer**, provided you like the specific Google team and product area. You successfully negotiated a top-tier package, solved the base pay gap, and gained an extra ~$184k/year in upside for the next two years.",
